 * Reprints & Permissions\n  * View PDF (open in a new window) PDF (open in a new window) View EPUB (open in a new window) EPUB (open in a new window)\n\nFormulae display:[Input]Image: MathJax Logo?Mathematical formulae have been encoded as MathML and are displayed in this HTML version using MathJax in order to improve their display. Uncheck the box to turn MathJax off. This feature requires Javascript. Click on a formula to zoom.\n\n## Abstract\n\nIn this paper we introduce some families of partitions of the set of natural numbers, Fibonacci-like partitions. In particular, we introduce a family of Fibonacci-like partitions into a number of parts corresponding to the Fibonacci numbers, the standard Fibonacci-like partitions of the first kind. Those partitions refine the well-known partition into two parts associated to the Wythoff sequences. In a similar way, we introduce another family of Fibonacci-like partitions of the natural numbers with an initial segment removed, into a number of parts which also follows the Fibonacci sequence, the standard Fibonacci-like partitions of the second kind.\n\nWe study some piecewise-defined permutations over Fibonacci-like partitions, which give both torsion-free and torsion elements in the symmetric group over the set of natural numbers.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNew upper bounds for the size of set systems with restricted intersections modulo prime powers (https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.02925)\nciteturn6academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.8 years ago; Title: New upper bounds for the size of set systems with restricted intersections modulo prime powers ... Let $q=p^\\alpha$ be a fixed prime power, $k\\geq 2$ be an integer.\nTitle: New upper bounds for the size of set systems with restricted intersections modulo prime powers\nAuthors: Gábor Hegedüs\nDate: Mon Jan  6 11:05:30 2025\n\nLet $q=p^\\alpha$ be a fixed prime power, $k\\geq 2$ be an integer. We give a new upper 